(43, 'MANEB', 'MSCE', 'Mathematics', 'Functions', NULL, 1.00, 150, NULL, NULL, NULL, '2026-07-04 08:03:30', '2026-07-04 08:03:30'),
(46, 'MANEB', 'MSCE', 'Mathematics', 'Other', NULL, 1.00, 160, NULL, NULL, NULL, '2026-07-04 08:03:31', '2026-07-04 08:03:31'),
(49, 'MANEB', 'MSCE', 'Mathematics', 'Irrational Numbers(Surds)', 'Surds are values left in radical form (\\(\\sqrt{x}\\)) because their exact value cannot be written as a fraction or a terminating/repeating decimal.They represent the exact roots of numbers (e.g., \\(\\sqrt{3}\\), \\(\\sqrt{5}\\), \\(\\sqrt{8}\\)).Keeping them as surds prevents rounding errors during multi-step calculations.', 1.00, 0, NULL, 10, 10, '2026-07-04 08:57:46', '2026-07-04 08:57:46'),
(50, 'MANEB', 'MSCE', 'Mathematics', 'Progressions AP and GP', NULL, 1.00, 0, NULL, 10, 10, '2026-07-04 09:03:13', '2026-07-04 09:03:13'),
(51, 'MANEB', 'MSCE', 'Mathematics', 'Sequences and Series', NULL, 1.00, 0, NULL, 10, 10, '2026-07-04 09:08:31', '2026-07-04 09:08:31'),
(52, 'MANEB', 'MSCE', 'Mathematics', 'Logarithms', 'This topic teaches how to manipulate expressions involving indices and logarithms. MSCE exams test your ability to use the laws of logarithms to combine terms, simplify expressions, change bases, and change the subject of the resulting algebraic formula', 1.00, 0, NULL, 10, 10, '2026-07-04 09:14:51', '2026-07-04 09:14:51'),
(54, 'MANEB', 'MSCE', 'Mathematics', 'Polynomials', 'This topic introduces techniques for working with higher-degree expressions like cubic polynomials. The Factor Theorem states that a linear expression \\((k - c)\\) is a factor of a polynomial \\(P(k)\\) if and only if \\(P(c) = 0\\). MSCE exams test your ability to apply this theorem to prove factors, find missing constants, and fully factorize cubic expressions.', 1.00, 0, NULL, 10, 10, '2026-07-04 12:00:12', '2026-07-04 12:00:12'),
(55, 'MANEB', 'MSCE', 'Mathematics', 'Linear Programming', 'This topic covers representing multiple constraints simultaneously on a coordinate grid to define a boundary region. In the MSCE syllabus, the standard examination convention is to shade the unwanted region, leaving the correct solution (known as the feasible region) completely clean and unshaded. It also tests the proper use of solid lines for inclusive inequalities (\\(\\le, \\ge\\)) and broken/dashed lines for strict inequalities (\\(<, >\\)).', 1.00, 0, NULL, 10, 10, '2026-07-04 12:07:10', '2026-07-04 12:07:10'),
(56, 'MANEB', 'MSCE', 'Mathematics', 'Variations', 'This topic covers mathematical relationships where a dependent variable changes based on the movement of two or more independent variables simultaneously. In joint variation, the relationship is expressed using a constant of variation (\\(k\\)). MSCE exams test your ability to use initial conditions to solve for \\(k\\) and then apply that constant to calculate an unknown variable value.', 1.00, 0, NULL, 10, 10, '2026-07-04 12:08:35', '2026-07-04 12:08:35'),
(57, 'MANEB', 'MSCE', 'Mathematics', 'Circle Geometry', 'This topic covers geometric proofs and properties related to lines, angles, and shapes drawn inside a circle. MSCE exams frequently test multiple geometric theorems simultaneously. In this problem, it requires combining the angle-in-a-semicircle theorem with the chord-bisector property.', 1.00, 0, NULL, 10, 10, '2026-07-04 12:11:55', '2026-07-04 12:11:55'),
(58, 'MANEB', 'MSCE', 'Mathematics', 'Transformation Geometry', 'This topic deals with changing the size of a shape on a coordinate grid while keeping its proportions. In the MSCE syllabus, you are tested on locating images using positive, fractional, and negative scale factors around a specific center point. A negative scale factor means the final shape will be flipped upside down and drawn on the opposite side of the center point.', 1.00, 0, NULL, 10, 10, '2026-07-04 12:13:05', '2026-07-04 12:13:05');
