(6, 1, 5, 19, 35, NULL, 'SmartLink Schools Pilot Syllabus File\nSample term-aligned syllabus for Reign International Academy and Happy Feet\nMontessori Preschool\nDesigned for syllabus upload, AI extraction, teacher review, Daily Drills, and parent\nprogress summaries.\n\nDocument Type\n\nSample syllabus upload file for SmartLink Schools\n\nSchool / Pilot Target\n\nReign International Academy and Happy Feet Montessori Preschool, Chileka\n\nCurriculum Track\n\nCambridge Primary-style starter plan + Montessori Early Years activity plan\n\nPilot Scope\n\n4-week starter syllabus for one primary class and one preschool group\n\nSubjects\n\nEnglish / Spelling, Mathematics, Science, Montessori Practical Life, Sensorial, Language, Math,\nCulture\n\nPrepared For\n\nSmartLink Schools pilot demonstration and term setup\n\nDate Prepared\n\n19 June 2026\n\nImportant note: This is a sample planning file, not an official Cambridge, Montessori, MANEB, or school-approved\nsyllabus. The school should review, edit, and approve all extracted topics before use with students.\n\nSmartLink Schools Pilot Syllabus Template - Sample / Not an official school curriculum\n\nPage 1\n\n1. Purpose and Upload Instructions\nThis PDF is structured so SmartLink Schools can read it, extract curriculum fields, and present them to a teacher/admin\nfor approval. It should be used as a pilot demonstration file or editable planning template.\nField\n\nValue SmartLink should extract\n\nSchool\n\nReign International Academy and Happy Feet Montessori Preschool\n\nAcademic Year\n\n2026/2027 or school-selected year\n\nTerm\n\nTerm 1 - new term pilot setup\n\nTerm Dates\n\nTo be confirmed by school: Start ________ End ________\n\nPilot Class\n\nCambridge Primary Stage 3 or nearest school-selected class\n\nPreschool Group\n\nHappy Feet Montessori Early Years\n\nApproval Rule\n\nAll topics, questions, answers, and explanations require teacher approval before student use\n\nSuggested extraction tags: curriculum, level, stage, subject, week, topic, subtopic, learning objective, skill, daily drill\nfocus, assessment note, parent summary note, confidence score.\n\n2. Term Setup Overview\nPeriod\n\nTerm Setup Goal\n\nSmartLink Output\n\nPre-term\n\nImport class list, teacher list, parent contacts, term dates,\nand syllabus plan.\n\nTerm dashboard, approved topic map, parent\ninvitation list.\n\nWeek 1\n\nBaseline checks, welcome updates, class routines,\nspelling/math readiness.\n\nBaseline mastery snapshot and first parent update.\n\nWeek 2\n\nStart Daily Drills from approved topics.\n\nStudent drill attempts and early weak-topic\ndetection.\n\nWeek 3\n\nContinue weekly topics and review weak areas.\n\nTeacher insight: class weak topics and missed drills.\n\nWeek 4\n\nMini progress review and parent summary.\n\nWeekly summary showing completed drills,\nstrengths, and support areas.\n\nSmartLink Schools Pilot Syllabus Template - Sample / Not an official school curriculum\n\nPage 2\n\n3. Happy Feet Montessori Early Years - 4 Week Starter Plan\nLevel: Montessori Early Years / Preschool. Focus: gentle development, observation, practical activities, language\nconfidence, numeracy foundations, and parent visibility. No high-pressure exam mode should be used.\nWeek\n\nTopic\n\nDetails for SmartLink extraction\n\n1\n\nPractical Life - Care of self\nand environment\n\nSubtopics: Hand washing, pouring, spooning, tidying learning space.\nObjectives: Follow simple routines; improve coordination; show independence.\nSkills: fine motor, independence, routine\nDaily Drill: Optional 3-item picture routine check.\nAssessment Notes: Teacher observation checklist.\n\n1\n\nLanguage - Listening and\noral expression\n\nSubtopics: Name objects, follow two-step instructions, story circle.\nObjectives: Listen attentively; answer simple questions; use new words.\nSkills: listening, vocabulary, speaking\nDaily Drill: Oral vocabulary game.\nAssessment Notes: Anecdotal notes; no formal scoring.\n\n2\n\nSensorial - Shapes, colours,\nand matching\n\nSubtopics: Sort by colour, match basic shapes, compare sizes.\nObjectives: Identify common colours and shapes; group similar items.\nSkills: classification, visual discrimination\nDaily Drill: Picture matching activity.\nAssessment Notes: Photo evidence or observation.\n\n2\n\nMathematics - Counting 1-10\n\nSubtopics: Count objects, match number symbols, compare more/less.\nObjectives: Count accurately with objects; recognise numbers 1-10.\nSkills: counting, one-to-one correspondence\nDaily Drill: Counting objects 1-10.\nAssessment Notes: Short activity check.\n\n3\n\nCulture - My school and\ncommunity\n\nSubtopics: People at school, classroom roles, helping others.\nObjectives: Identify school helpers; practise respectful behaviour.\nSkills: social awareness, confidence\nDaily Drill: Match helper to role.\nAssessment Notes: Participation notes.\n\n3\n\nLanguage - Phonics\nreadiness\n\nSubtopics: Initial sounds, rhymes, picture-word matching.\nObjectives: Hear beginning sounds; match simple picture sounds.\nSkills: phonemic awareness\nDaily Drill: Initial sound picture drill.\nAssessment Notes: Simple teacher-led oral check.\n\n4\n\nPractical Life - Grace and\ncourtesy\n\nSubtopics: Greeting, sharing materials, waiting turn, saying thank you.\nObjectives: Practise polite classroom behaviour and turn-taking.\nSkills: social skills, self-regulation\nDaily Drill: Scenario choice activity.\nAssessment Notes: Observation summary for guardian.\n\n4\n\nMathematics - Patterns and\nsorting\n\nSubtopics: AB patterns, object sorting by size/colour/type.\nObjectives: Continue simple patterns; explain sorting rule.\nSkills: patterning, reasoning\nDaily Drill: Finish the pattern.\nAssessment Notes: Practical task completion.\n\nSmartLink Schools Pilot Syllabus Template - Sample / Not an official school curriculum\n\nPage 3\n\n4. Cambridge Primary Stage 3 - English and Spelling\nLevel: Cambridge Primary-style Stage 3 starter plan. Subject: English / Spelling. Focus: reading fluency, vocabulary,\ngrammar, spelling patterns, and simple writing.\nWeek\n\nTopic\n\nDetails for SmartLink extraction\n\n1\n\nReading routines and\ncomprehension\n\nSubtopics: Short stories, main idea, who/what/where questions.\nObjectives: Read a short text and answer literal questions; identify main character and\nsetting.\nSkills: reading, recall, comprehension\nDaily Drill: 5 short comprehension questions.\nAssessment Notes: Baseline reading check.\n\n1\n\nSpelling list 1\n\nSubtopics: Short vowel words and common classroom words.\nObjectives: Spell selected weekly words correctly and use them in oral sentences.\nSkills: spelling, vocabulary\nDaily Drill: 5-word spelling drill.\nAssessment Notes: Friday spelling check.\n\n2\n\nSentence building\n\nSubtopics: Capital letters, full stops, simple sentence order.\nObjectives: Write clear simple sentences using correct punctuation.\nSkills: grammar, writing\nDaily Drill: Fix-the-sentence questions.\nAssessment Notes: Teacher marks short written responses.\n\n2\n\nVocabulary growth\n\nSubtopics: Synonyms, antonyms, word meaning from context.\nObjectives: Use context to understand common words; choose suitable alternatives.\nSkills: vocabulary, reasoning\nDaily Drill: Multiple choice word meaning.\nAssessment Notes: Low-stakes class quiz.\n\n3\n\nGrammar basics\n\nSubtopics: Nouns, verbs, adjectives.\nObjectives: Identify nouns, verbs, and adjectives in simple sentences.\nSkills: grammar, classification\nDaily Drill: Label word type in sentences.\nAssessment Notes: Auto-mark objective items.\n\n3\n\nSpelling list 2\n\nSubtopics: Consonant blends and common high-frequency words.\nObjectives: Spell weekly words and recognise patterns.\nSkills: spelling, phonics\nDaily Drill: Spelling + sentence completion.\nAssessment Notes: Friday spelling check.\n\n4\n\nWriting short paragraphs\n\nSubtopics: Topic sentence, supporting details, neat ending.\nObjectives: Write a short paragraph about school/home using 3-5 sentences.\nSkills: composition, organisation\nDaily Drill: Choose best sentence order.\nAssessment Notes: Teacher review for writing.\n\n5. Sample English / Spelling Question Seeds\nTopic\n\nQuestion / Answer\n\nExplanation\n\nSpelling List 1\n\nType: short_answer\nQuestion: Spell the word: school\nCorrect answer: school\n\nThe word school begins with sch and ends\nwith ool.\n\nGrammar Basics\n\nType: multiple_choice\nQuestion: Which word is a verb? A. jump B. blue C. table\nCorrect answer: A\n\nA verb is an action word. Jump is an action.\n\nSentence Building\n\nType: short_answer\nQuestion: Rewrite correctly: my name is ana.\nCorrect answer: My name is Ana.\n\nA sentence starts with a capital letter and\nnames start with capital letters.\n\nSmartLink Schools Pilot Syllabus Template - Sample / Not an official school curriculum\n\nPage 4\n\n6. Cambridge Primary Stage 3 - Mathematics\nSubject: Mathematics. Focus: number confidence, operations, measurement, shapes, and problem solving.\nWeek\n\nTopic\n\nDetails for SmartLink extraction\n\n1\n\nNumber and place value\n\nSubtopics: Counting, ordering, tens and ones, compare numbers.\nObjectives: Read, write, order, and compare numbers within the class range.\nSkills: number sense, comparison\nDaily Drill: 8 number questions.\nAssessment Notes: Baseline number check.\n\n1\n\nAddition and subtraction\nreview\n\nSubtopics: Mental addition, subtraction, missing numbers.\nObjectives: Add and subtract simple numbers using mental or written strategies.\nSkills: calculation, fluency\nDaily Drill: Mixed addition/subtraction.\nAssessment Notes: Auto-mark objective items.\n\n2\n\nMultiplication foundations\n\nSubtopics: Repeated addition, 2x, 5x, 10x facts.\nObjectives: Represent multiplication as equal groups and repeated addition.\nSkills: multiplication, reasoning\nDaily Drill: Times-table fluency.\nAssessment Notes: Teacher checks working.\n\n2\n\nWord problems\n\nSubtopics: One-step problems using addition/subtraction.\nObjectives: Choose the correct operation for a simple word problem.\nSkills: problem-solving\nDaily Drill: Story problem daily drill.\nAssessment Notes: Track wrong-operation mistakes.\n\n3\n\nMeasurement\n\nSubtopics: Length, mass, capacity, choosing units.\nObjectives: Use appropriate units and compare measurements.\nSkills: measurement, estimation\nDaily Drill: Unit choice and comparison.\nAssessment Notes: Practical measurement task.\n\n3\n\nGeometry\n\nSubtopics: 2D shapes, sides, corners, symmetry intro.\nObjectives: Identify common shapes and describe simple properties.\nSkills: geometry, classification\nDaily Drill: Shape property questions.\nAssessment Notes: Auto-mark MCQ.\n\n4\n\nFractions introduction\n\nSubtopics: Halves, quarters, equal parts, simple diagrams.\nObjectives: Recognise halves and quarters as equal parts of a whole.\nSkills: fractions, visual reasoning\nDaily Drill: Picture-based fractions.\nAssessment Notes: Teacher review for diagrams.\n\n7. Sample Mathematics Question Seeds\nTopic\n\nQuestion / Answer\n\nExplanation\n\nPlace Value\n\nType: multiple_choice\nQuestion: What is the value of 5 in 352? A. 5 B. 50 C. 500\nCorrect answer: B\n\nIn 352, the 5 is in the tens place, so its value is\n50.\n\nAddition\n\nType: short_answer\nQuestion: 27 + 15 = ?\nCorrect answer: 42\n\nAdd ones first, then tens. 27 + 15 = 42.\n\nFractions\n\nType: multiple_choice\nQuestion: Which fraction means one out of two equal\nparts? A. 1/4 B. 1/2 C. 2/3\nCorrect answer: B\n\nOne out of two equal parts is one half, written\nas 1/2.\n\nSmartLink Schools Pilot Syllabus Template - Sample / Not an official school curriculum\n\nPage 5\n\n8. Cambridge Primary Stage 3 - Science\nSubject: Science. Focus: observation, questioning, living things, materials, forces, and simple scientific enquiry.\nWeek\n\nTopic\n\nDetails for SmartLink extraction\n\n1\n\nLiving things\n\nSubtopics: Plants and animals, basic needs, habitats.\nObjectives: Describe what living things need and identify simple habitats.\nSkills: observation, classification\nDaily Drill: Living/non-living questions.\nAssessment Notes: Class discussion + quiz.\n\n1\n\nScientific enquiry\n\nSubtopics: Observe, predict, record.\nObjectives: Make simple observations and record findings using words or pictures.\nSkills: enquiry, recording\nDaily Drill: Observation vocabulary.\nAssessment Notes: Practical observation task.\n\n2\n\nPlants\n\nSubtopics: Parts of a plant, roots, stem, leaves, flowers.\nObjectives: Name common parts of a plant and describe simple functions.\nSkills: recall, explanation\nDaily Drill: Plant part matching.\nAssessment Notes: Auto-mark matching/MCQ.\n\n2\n\nMaterials\n\nSubtopics: Wood, plastic, metal, glass, fabric.\nObjectives: Identify materials and describe properties such as hard, soft, smooth, rough.\nSkills: classification, vocabulary\nDaily Drill: Material property questions.\nAssessment Notes: Practical sorting activity.\n\n3\n\nForces and movement\n\nSubtopics: Push, pull, movement, simple speed/direction changes.\nObjectives: Describe pushes and pulls and observe how objects move.\nSkills: cause-effect, observation\nDaily Drill: Push/pull examples.\nAssessment Notes: Teacher-led practical notes.\n\n3\n\nHealth and hygiene\n\nSubtopics: Clean hands, food, exercise, rest.\nObjectives: Explain basic habits that keep the body healthy.\nSkills: health literacy\nDaily Drill: Short scenario questions.\nAssessment Notes: Parent summary note.\n\n4\n\nEarth and weather\n\nSubtopics: Weather observation, clouds, rain, sun, wind.\nObjectives: Observe daily weather and use simple weather words.\nSkills: observation, recording\nDaily Drill: Weather vocabulary.\nAssessment Notes: Mini weather diary.\n\n9. Sample Science Question Seeds\nTopic\n\nQuestion / Answer\n\nExplanation\n\nLiving Things\n\nType: multiple_choice\nQuestion: Which one is a living thing? A. stone B. goat C.\nchair\nCorrect answer: B\n\nA goat is living because it grows, needs food,\nand can reproduce.\n\nPlants\n\nType: short_answer\nQuestion: Which part of a plant takes in water from the\nsoil?\nCorrect answer: roots\n\nRoots help the plant take in water and hold it in\nthe soil.\n\nMaterials\n\nType: multiple_choice\nQuestion: Which material is usually transparent? A. glass\nB. wood C. cloth\nCorrect answer: A\n\nTransparent means light can pass through it.\nGlass is usually transparent.\n\nSmartLink Schools Pilot Syllabus Template - Sample / Not an official school curriculum\n\nPage 6', 0, 'reign_happy_feet_pilot_syllabus_sample_extracted.txt', '2026-06-19 17:11:36');
INSERT INTO `syllabus_document_chunks` (`id`, `school_id`, `upload_id`, `subject_id`, `grade_id`, `topic_id`, `chunk_text`, `chunk_index`, `source_filename`, `created_at`) VALUES
(7, 1, 5, 19, 35, NULL, 'ds\nTopic\n\nQuestion / Answer\n\nExplanation\n\nLiving Things\n\nType: multiple_choice\nQuestion: Which one is a living thing? A. stone B. goat C.\nchair\nCorrect answer: B\n\nA goat is living because it grows, needs food,\nand can reproduce.\n\nPlants\n\nType: short_answer\nQuestion: Which part of a plant takes in water from the\nsoil?\nCorrect answer: roots\n\nRoots help the plant take in water and hold it in\nthe soil.\n\nMaterials\n\nType: multiple_choice\nQuestion: Which material is usually transparent? A. glass\nB. wood C. cloth\nCorrect answer: A\n\nTransparent means light can pass through it.\nGlass is usually transparent.\n\nSmartLink Schools Pilot Syllabus Template - Sample / Not an official school curriculum\n\nPage 6\n\n10. Daily Drill Rules for This Pilot\nDaily Drills should be short, teacher-approved, and aligned with weekly topics. SmartLink should not use unapproved\nAI-generated questions with students.\nClass / Level\n\nDrill Length\n\nSubjects\n\nAI Use\n\nMontessori Early Years\n\n3-5 guided items, optional\n\nPractical Life, Sensorial,\nLanguage, Math\n\nAI may draft teacher activities only;\nno direct child AI tutor.\n\nCambridge Primary Stage 3\n\n5-8 questions, 5-10\nminutes\n\nEnglish, Spelling, Mathematics,\nScience\n\nAI may simplify approved\nexplanations after student asks for\nhelp.\n\n11. Assessment and Parent Summary Plan\nAssessment Type\n\nWhen\n\nPurpose\n\nSmartLink Record\n\nBaseline check\n\nWeek 1\n\nFind starting level in spelling, reading, number, and\nscience vocabulary.\n\nbaseline_assessments and\nmastery snapshot\n\nDaily Drill\n\nWeek 2 onward\n\nLow-pressure practice and weak-topic detection.\n\ndrill_sessions and\nstudent_topic_mastery\n\nFriday spelling check\n\nWeekly\n\nTrack weekly spelling progress.\n\nquestion_bank + weekly\nresult\n\nTeacher observation\n\nOngoing\n\nCapture Montessori practical and social\ndevelopment.\n\nteacher observation note\n\nParent summary\n\nEnd of week\n\nCommunicate progress without shaming learners.\n\nguardian weekly drill\nsummary\n\nParent summary example: Your child completed 4 of 5 practice activities this week. Strong area: number recognition.\nSupport area: spelling short vowel words. Recommended home activity: read one short story together and practise five\nwords for 10 minutes.\n\n12. Teacher Approval Checklist\nItem\n\nApprove?\n\nTerm dates entered correctly\n\nYes / No\n\nClass/stage selected correctly\n\nYes / No\n\nSubjects match school plan\n\nYes / No\n\nWeekly topics match teacher scheme of work\n\nYes / No\n\nDaily Drill questions are age-appropriate\n\nYes / No\n\nAnswers and explanations are correct\n\nYes / No\n\nParent summary wording is non-shaming\n\nYes / No\n\nSmartLink Schools Pilot Syllabus Template - Sample / Not an official school curriculum\n\nNotes\n\nPage 7\n\n13. SmartLink Extraction Schema\nSmartLink should extract the following fields from this PDF and present them to the school for approval.\nSchema Field\n\nExpected Value\n\nschool\n\nReign International Academy and Happy Feet Montessori Preschool\n\nterm\n\nTerm 1 or school-confirmed term\n\npilot_duration_weeks\n\n4\n\ntrack_1.level\n\nMontessori Early Years\n\ntrack_1.subjects\n\nPractical Life, Sensorial, Language, Mathematics, Culture\n\ntrack_2.level\n\nCambridge Primary Stage 3\n\ntrack_2.subjects\n\nEnglish, Spelling, Mathematics, Science\n\napproval_required\n\ntrue\n\nstudent_use_requires_approved_questi\nons\n\ntrue\n\n14. Approval Sign-off\nRole\n\nName\n\nSignature\n\nDate\n\nSchool Director / Headteacher\nPilot Teacher\nSmartLink Representative\n\nRomeo Mbeya\n\n15. References Used to Build This Sample\nThis document is a sample and does not copy official Cambridge assessment papers or proprietary school materials. It\nuses public curriculum descriptions only for broad structure.\nCambridge Primary curriculum: Cambridge International states that Cambridge Primary offers over ten subjects including English, mathematics\nand science, with opportunities for creativity, expression and wellbeing. Source:\nhttps://www.cambridgeinternational.org/programmes-and-qualifications/cambridge-primary/curriculum\nCambridge Primary assessment: Cambridge Progression Tests are internal classroom assessments for checking knowledge, skills and\nunderstanding, available for core subjects including English, mathematics and science. Source:\nhttps://www.cambridgeinternational.org/programmes-and-qualifications/cambridge-primary/assessment/\nMontessori early childhood structure: Montessori early childhood curriculum is commonly organised around Practical Life, Sensorial, Math,\nLanguage, and Cultural Studies. Source: https://amshq.org/about-us/inside-the-montessori-classroom/early-childhood/\n\nSmartLink Schools Pilot Syllabus Template - Sample / Not an official school curriculum\n\nPage 8', 1, 'reign_happy_feet_pilot_syllabus_sample_extracted.txt', '2026-06-19 17:11:36'),
(44, 3, 10, 65, 76, NULL, 'AGRICULTURE\n       TEACHING SYLLABUS\nPRIMARY SCHOOL — STANDARD 3 (GRADE 3)\n\n8 Units | 3 Terms | Full Academic Year\n\nPrepared for classroom use in the Malawian primary school context\n\nACKNOWLEDGEMENTS\nThe development of this Grade 3 Agriculture Teaching Syllabus has been a collaborative effort, and gratitude is\nextended to everyone who contributed their time, expertise, and insight to its completion.\nSincere appreciation goes to the primary school teachers whose classroom experience shaped the practical, age-\nappropriate approach taken throughout this syllabus. Their understanding of how young learners engage with\nagricultural concepts, and their commitment to hands-on, experiential teaching, has been invaluable in ensuring the\ncontent is both realistic and achievable within a typical school term.\nThanks are also due to agricultural extension officers and community farmers who generously shared local\nknowledge of crop cycles, livestock practices, and seasonal farming activities specific to the Malawian context.\nTheir input ensured that the examples, crops, and practices referenced in this syllabus reflect the lived agricultural\nenvironment of the learners it is intended to serve.\nAppreciation is extended to curriculum development specialists whose guidance on structuring units, topics, aims,\nand success criteria helped shape a syllabus that is both pedagogically sound and easy for teachers to follow and\nadapt.\nGratitude also goes to the school administrators and head teachers who recognise the value of practical agricultural\neducation in building life skills, food security awareness, and environmental responsibility among young learners,\nand who continue to support garden-based learning within their schools.\nFinally, this work is dedicated to the learners themselves — the young farmers, gardeners, and stewards of the land\nwho will carry forward what they learn in these lessons into their homes, their communities, and, for some, into a\nlifelong relationship with the land.\nIt is hoped that this syllabus will serve as a practical and inspiring guide for teachers as they nurture curiosity,\nresponsibility, and a genuine appreciation for agriculture in every learner.\n\nINTRODUCTION\n\nRationale\nAgriculture is central to the livelihood, food security, and economy of Malawi. Introducing learners to agricultural\nconcepts at an early age builds practical life skills, an appreciation for where food comes from, and a foundation of\nenvironmental responsibility. This syllabus is designed for Standard 3 (Grade 3) learners and combines simple\ntheoretical knowledge with hands-on, garden-based activities suited to their developmental stage.\n\nGeneral Aims of the Syllabus\n    ●   To develop learners\' basic knowledge and understanding of agricultural practices relevant to their\n        community.\n    ●   To build practical skills in crop production, animal care, and safe use of simple farm tools.\n    ●   To foster positive attitudes towards farming, food security, and environmental conservation.\n    ●   To connect classroom learning with real, hands-on activity through the school and home garden.\n    ●   To lay a foundation of agricultural literacy that learners will build on in later grades.\n\nTeaching and Learning Methodology\nLessons should be predominantly practical and activity-based, making use of the school garden, real tools, and\nlocally available samples wherever possible. Recommended approaches include:\n    ●   Demonstration followed by guided practice\n    ●   Small-group and pair activities in the garden\n    ●   Discussion and question-and-answer sessions\n    ●   Use of real objects, pictures, and simple charts\n    ●   Short field observations within the school compound or nearby farms\n\nAssessment Strategies\nAssessment should be continuous and largely practical, appropriate to the age of the learners. Suggested methods\ninclude:\n    ●   Oral questions and class discussion\n    ●   Observation of practical garden tasks\n    ●   Simple written or picture-based exercises\n    ●   Short end-of-unit checks against the stated success criteria\n    ●   A simple end-of-term practical project (e.g. a class garden bed)\n\nDuration and Structure\nThis syllabus contains 8 units spread across a full academic year of 3 terms. A suggested pacing guide is provided\nbelow; teachers should adjust timing to suit their local school calendar and available garden space.\n\nSYLLABUS OVERVIEW\n\nTerm     Units Covered                                   Focus\n\nTerm 1   Unit 1: Introduction to Agriculture             Foundations and getting the\n         Unit 2: The School and Home Garden              garden started\n         Unit 3: Crop Production\n\nTerm 2   Unit 4: Soil and Water                          Resources, animals, and safe\n         Unit 5: Livestock and Animal Care               practice\n         Unit 6: Farm Tools and Safety\n\nTerm 3   Unit 7: Agriculture and the Environment         Conservation, nutrition, and\n         Unit 8: Food from the Farm                      consolidation\n         Revision and end-of-year practical project\n\nUNIT 1: INTRODUCTION TO AGRICULTURE\n\n1.1 Meaning and Importance of Agriculture\n   ● Meaning of agriculture\n   ● Agriculture in our daily lives\n   ● Why we grow crops and keep animals\nTo help learners understand what agriculture is and why it is important to families and the community.\n   ● Learners can explain in their own words what agriculture means\n   ● Learners can name three reasons why agriculture is important\n   ● Learners can give examples of agricultural activities happening around their home\n\n1.2 Types of Agricultural Activities\n   ● Crop farming\n   ● Livestock keeping\n   ● Fishing\n   ● Forestry\nTo introduce learners to the different branches of agriculture practised in Malawi.\n   ● Learners can identify and name the four main types of agricultural activities\n   ● Learners can match pictures of farming activities to their correct type\n   ● Learners can describe one activity from each type\n\nUNIT 2: THE SCHOOL AND HOME GARDEN\n\n2.1 Preparing a Garden\n   ● Choosing a garden site\n   ● Clearing the land\n   ● Digging and loosening the soil\n   ● Making garden beds and ridges\nTo equip learners with basic skills for preparing land for planting.\n   ● Learners can describe the steps taken to prepare a garden\n   ● Learners can help clear a small plot of land safely\n   ● Learners can make a simple garden bed with guidance\n\n2.2 Planting\n   ● Choosing good seeds\n   ● Spacing of seeds and seedlings\n   ● Depth of planting\n   ● Best time of planting\nTo teach learners correct methods of planting seeds and seedlings.\n   ● Learners can plant seeds at the correct spacing and depth\n   ● Learners can explain why spacing is important\n   ● Learners can identify good seeds from poor seeds\n\nUNIT 3: CROP PRODUCTION\n\n3.1 Types of Crops\n   ● Food crops (maize, beans, cassava, sweet potatoes)\n   ● Cash crops (tobacco, cotton, groundnuts)\n   ● Vegetables and fruits\nTo help learners identify and classify crops grown in Malawi.\n   ● Learners can name at least five crops grown in their community\n   ● Learners can sort crops into food crops and cash crops\n   ● Learners can identify crops from pictures or real samples\n\n3.2 Caring for Crops\n   ● Weeding\n   ● Watering\n   ● Protecting crops from pests\n   ● Applying manure and fertiliser\nTo develop learners\' understanding of how to care for growing crops.\n   ● Learners can explain why weeding is important\n   ● Learners can demonstrate correct watering of a garden bed\n   ● Learners can identify common pests that damage crops\n\n3.3 Harvesting and Storage\n   ● Signs that a crop is ready for harvest\n   ● Harvesting methods\n   ● Simple storage methods\nTo teach learners when and how crops are harvested and kept safe after harvest.\n   ● Learners can describe signs that show a crop is ready to harvest\n   ● Learners can explain simple ways of storing harvested crops\n   ● Learners can state why proper storage prevents food loss\n\nUNIT 4: SOIL AND WATER\n\n4.1 Types and Properties of Soil\n   ● Sandy soil\n   ● Clay soil\n   ● Loam soil\n   ● Which soil is best for crops\nTo help learners recognise different soil types and their suitability for farming.\n   ● Learners can describe the feel and colour of at least two soil types\n   ● Learners can identify soil samples brought from home\n   ● Learners can explain why loam soil is good for most crops\n\n4.2 Keeping Soil Healthy\n   ● Adding manure and compost\n   ● The idea of crop rotation\n   ● Avoiding soil erosion\nTo introduce learners to simple practices that keep soil fertile and healthy.\n   ● Learners can explain what manure does to soil\n   ● Learners can describe one way to stop soil from washing away\n   ● Learners can state why farmers should not plant the same crop every season\n\n4.3 Water for Farming\n   ● Sources of water for crops\n   ● Ways of watering a garden\n   ● Saving water\nTo teach learners the role of water in growing healthy crops and animals.\n   ● Learners can name at least two sources of water for farming\n   ● Learners can demonstrate a safe way of watering plants\n   ● Learners can suggest one way of saving water in the garden\n\nUNIT 5: LIVESTOCK AND ANIMAL CARE\n\n5.1 Domestic Animals\n   ● Types of domestic animals (chickens, goats, cattle, pigs)\n   ● Products we get from animals\nTo introduce learners to common domestic animals kept in their community and their uses.\n   ● Learners can name at least four domestic animals kept at home\n   ● Learners can list products obtained from these animals, such as milk, eggs, and meat\n   ● Learners can match an animal to the product it provides\n\n5.2 Caring for Animals\n   ● Feeding animals\n   ● Providing shelter\n   ● Keeping animals clean and healthy\n   ● Water for animals\nTo help learners understand the basic needs of domestic animals.\n   ● Learners can state the basic needs of a farm animal\n   ● Learners can describe how to keep an animal shelter clean\n   ● Learners can explain why animals need clean water daily\n\nUNIT 6: FARM TOOLS AND SAFETY\n\n6.1 Common Farm Tools\n   ● Hoe\n   ● Panga knife (machete)\n   ● Watering can\n   ● Wheelbarrow\n   ● Rake\nTo familiarise learners with common farm tools and their correct uses.\n   ● Learners can name and identify at least five farm tools\n   ● Learners can match each tool to the task it is used for\n   ● Learners can demonstrate the correct way of holding a hoe\n\n6.2 Safety on the Farm\n   ● Handling sharp tools carefully\n   ● Wearing appropriate clothing\n   ● Washing hands after garden work\n   ● Keeping tools away from young children\nTo develop safety awareness when learners work in the garden or handle farm tools.\n   ● Learners can list three safety rules to follow when using farm tools\n   ● Learners can explain why they must wash their hands after garden work\n   ● Learners can demonstrate safe carrying of a garden tool\n\nUNIT 7: AGRICULTURE AND THE ENVIRONMENT\n\n7.1 Trees and Forests\n   ● Importance of trees\n   ● Planting trees\n   ● Effects of cutting down trees\nTo help learners appreciate the value of trees and forests to agriculture and life.\n   ● Learners can give three reasons why trees are important\n   ● Learners can take part in planting a tree at school\n   ● Learners can explain one bad effect of cutting down too many trees\n\n7.2 Caring for the Environment\n   ● Avoiding bush fires\n   ● Proper disposal of rubbish\n   ● Protecting water sources\n   ● Simple conservation practices\nTo instil good environmental practices that support sustainable agriculture.\n   ● Learners can state why bush fires are harmful\n   ● Learners can describe one way to keep a water source clean\n   ● Learners can identify one action they can take to protect their environment\n\nUNIT 8: FOOD FROM THE FARM\n\n8.1 From Farm to Table\n   ● How food travels from garden to kitchen\n   ● Washing and preparing farm food\n   ● Reducing food waste\nTo help learners trace the journey of food from the farm to their plate.\n   ● Learners can describe the steps food takes from farm to table\n   ● Learners can explain why food should be washed before eating\n   ● Learners can suggest one way to avoid wasting food at home\n\n8.2 Healthy Eating from the Farm\n   ● Farm foods that keep us healthy\n   ● Balanced meals using local produce\n   ● Growing a simple kitchen garden\nTo connect agricultural produce with good nutrition and healthy living.\n   ● Learners can name farm foods that are good for their bodies\n   ● Learners can describe what a balanced plate of local food looks like\n   ● Learners can suggest three foods to grow in a small kitchen garden', 0, 'Grade3_Agriculture_Syllabus.pdf', '2026-07-02 08:56:11'),
(47, 3, 12, 53, 77, NULL, 'Introduction\nOCR involves teachers in the development of new support materials to capture current teaching practices\ntailored to our new specifications. These support materials are designed to inspire teachers and facilitate\ndifferent ideas and teaching practices. Each Scheme of Work is provided in Word format – so that you can\nuse it as a foundation to build upon and amend the content to suit your teaching style and students’ needs.\n\nThe Scheme of Work provides examples of how to teach this unit and the teaching hours are suggestions\nonly. Some or all of it may be applicable to your teaching.\n\nThe Specification is the document on which assessment is based and specifies what content and skills\nneed to be covered in delivering the course. At all times, therefore, this Support Material booklet should be\nread in conjunction with the Specification. If clarification on a particular point is sought then that clarification\nshould be found in the Specification itself.\n\n© OCR​                                                ​                                                           V1\nINSERT INTO `syllabus_extracted_items` (`id`, `upload_id`, `school_id`, `item_type`, `parent_extracted_item_id`, `title`, `description`, `term`, `suggested_week`, `exam_relevance`, `keywords_json`, `confidence`, `status`, `approved_by`, `approved_at`, `merged_into_topic_id`, `raw_json`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
